WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.630
This is a teasmade.
And it is the most British invention.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:07.820
It's a combination alarm clock and electric kettle,
which means it has two settings:

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.220
alarm,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.660
and alarm with tea.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.350
And like a lot of clocks that are
built into appliances,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:17.150
so, clocks on ovens and microwaves,
and old style clock radios,

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.529
and central heating systems,

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:24.810
this clock keeps time by using the frequency
of the power grid that it's connected to.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.849
Or would be connected to, anyway, obviously
I don't have power out here.

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:35.000
Power grids on this side of the Atlantic run at
a frequency of 50 hertz, fifty cycles a second.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.930
Now that 50 hertz frequency might
rise or fall by a tiny fraction

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:43.050
as supply and demand affect the grid, but
over the course of days and weeks

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.280
the electricity companies guarantee that it'll
be averaged out to 50 hertz.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.940
So clocks like this, which have to be plugged
into the grid anyway,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.530
can use that regular 50-cycles-a-second beat
to keep themselves accurate.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:00.300
Set this to the correct time, and it'll keep
the correct time as long as the power's on.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.360
Except.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.120
Over the last month, clocks over in Europe
have lost six minutes.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.200
If they're connected to the
Continental European Power System,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.770
which is one connected, synchronised grid stretching
all the way from Spain to Denmark to Turkey,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.530
if that clock is connected to that grid,
it's six minutes slow.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.240
This was revealed in a press release a few
days ago from the

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.689
European transmission operators' association,

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:27.990
who managed to write a press release that
sounded vaguely like

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.600
an angry parent who's getting close to the
end of their rope.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.880
The problem is politics.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.810
Serbia and Kosovo have
fallen out with each other.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.030
That is an enormous simplification,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.469
but if you think I'm going to try and explain
the history of the Balkan politics system

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:46.159
while holding a teasmade,
er, you are sorely mistaken.

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:49.429
Kosovo is low on power,
Serbia isn't helping manage it,

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:53.240
and as a result the entire European grid has
been slightly underpowered

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.240
since the middle of January.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.960
Not by much -- but enough that the average
grid frequency

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.590
has dropped from 50 hertz to 49.996.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.490
So clocks like this, who are expecting 50...

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.420
okay, it doesn't sound like much,

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.420
but it works out to them losing
six minutes over the last couple of months.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.480
Problem number one is getting the grid
back up to frequency,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.700
and hopefully politics will solve that.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.600
But problem number two is: when it is fixed,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.030
should the European authorities
bring the grid back to 50?

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.500
Or should they run it slightly fast
for a few weeks?

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.900
If they do, it'll bring everything back that
wasn't manually corrected...

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.040
but anyone who has already changed their clocks

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.610
will then find them
starting to run six minutes fast.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.350
And they'll have to change them again.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.870
They're probably going to pick
the second option, but who knows?

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.200
Fortunately for me,
I don't have to worry about it.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.610
As I'm sure 52% of Britons
will be ecstatic to hear,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.310
the mainland European grid is not synchronised
to the British National Grid here.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.550
It is connected, through two undersea cables,

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.680
one under the English Channel from France,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.380
and the other one from the Netherlands,
landing right here,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.530
on the Isle of Grain, which is near...

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.320
nowhere. It's... it's absolutely
in the middle of nowhere.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.310
But these cables are direct current cables,
not alternating current.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.030
They doesn't require grid synchronisation.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.180
So while mainland Europe's grid is slow,

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.390
our British grid is going
at 50 British cycles a second,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.740
as Her Majesty intended.
["God Save the Queen" plays]

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.550
Our... our ovens are turning on
at the right time.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.700
Our clock radios are remaining on time.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.720
Our... our microwaves are
showing the correct time!

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.980
[Music stops] Or they're showing 12:00 because
no-one's bothered to program them,

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.880
but that's not the point, the point is,
[Music resumes]

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.010
the clocks may be going slow all over Europe,
but here in Britain,

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.560
our teasmades are boiling precisely on schedule.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.950
God Save the Queen.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.700
And god help us all.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.320
Good!

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.780
I think that's a take.

